# A committed TEMPLATE of .github/c2-serving-job.env for the tp4/s7 window (the ship-7 candidate; scripts/ci/c2_serving_job.py parses it): copy it over
# .github/c2-serving-job.env on a throwaway commit of tp4/s7 and push a tag experiment/c2-serving-vN, ONE TAG AT A TIME (wait for the run to finish; never cancel a running card job).
# Nothing here names a rig, a card, a host or a registry: the cards are resolved at run time. The jobs are templates only: no tag is pushed from the branch by its author. ORDER.txt and
# ORDER-SHIP.txt have the order, the stop rules, the minutes and the read rules of the two runs.
# ONE IMAGE FOR THE WHOLE WINDOW AND THE SHIP: C2_IMAGE_TAG=tp4-s7-1 is a PLACEHOLDER for the tag B0 builds from the pushed branch head WITH the K64j-OQ graft and the ship-7 profile baked as the serving default
# (sed -i 's/tp4-s7-1/<tag>/' *.env). A commit of a file the image carries needs a new B0 build and a new tag in every template. This window never stops, starts or hands back the node agent.
# C2_MODEL_REPO / C2_MODEL_REVISION (qwen_c2_checkpoint.py): a job that carries them serves the Swift checkpoint (what production serves) from its own converted-weight directory; the keys are the same in every
# smoke and prefix job of this pack, so each pair differs by the profile alone. The replay and build steps do not forward the selection (the job reader refuses the keys beside them): they run the base checkpoint.
# T1B (TIMED, the CANDIDATE of the T1 pair): the ship-7 candidate, a TRAFFIC profile under the owner waiver the ship-7 decision records (a PENDING one boots in a smoke and
# logs the word) c2-packed-tp4-8x262k-ship-prefix-levern-w2-er-fx-traffic-s7 on Swift. Tests: np1_live1, np1_live2 and np1_live4 (one, two and four users started together
# on ~3,500-token real-code prompts, every answer to EXACTLY 2,000 tokens with ignore_eos); prefill_few_decoders (the governor's shape); concurrent8_steady (eight users:
# the octo block); agent_turns last (the main judge: returning turns with tails, restores, rebinds and the lookup). prefill_few_decoders (ONE decoder and a ~120k cold
# arrival, then TWO decoders and a ~254k one): per shape the arrival's first-token time and prefill rate, each decoder's longest gap from the arrival on and its progress
# inside the arrival's window. ESTIMATE (docs/lever-n-adaptive-governor.md, from the timing model, never measured at 254k): the candidate's first token comes 31 to 44
# percent sooner (254k: about 182-187 s to 114-119 s; 120k: about 85 s to 50 s) and the decoders get about a fifth of the control's rate inside that window. Read the pair
# with `python3 scripts/ci/prefill_ladder_report.py <T1A smoke log> --second <T1B smoke log>`; the answers of the cold arrivals must hash EQUAL between the arms (greedy
# text, only WHEN the rounds run moves). agent_turns (6 sessions of 64k, 128k and 200k tokens, 12 returning turns each) is the MAIN JUDGE: the ship-5 window's T1B number
# (50.36 tok/s aggregate, returning turn first token p50 1.46 s, p90 2.33 s) is its baseline. agent_turns8 is EIGHT sessions weighted like production traffic (contexts of
# 24k to 80k tokens and one of ~200k, about 550k tokens in all, mean ~69k against production's ~59k; answers capped at 300 to 1,080 tokens, mean 690 like production's; the
# same gaps and 12 turns), every seat busy, no new turn after 25 minutes, an estimate of 15 minutes: the production load, where WC (2 or 4 users) and the governor (1 or 2
# decoders) mostly sit out, so the pair says whether anything is LOST there. Compare it with its own control leg, never with agent_turns. Read both legs with `python3
# scripts/ci/agent_turns.py report --smoke-log <smoke> --container-log <container> --test <name>`: the rates, the TTFT by context bucket, the tokens re-prefilled, the
# per-live table; the per-turn answer hashes must be EQUAL between the legs. Every boot a fresh reset. READ (T1B): c2_smoke_check clean on both legs and octo_judge.py clean
# on the candidate's log; then python3 scripts/ci/np1_report.py --control-a <T1A smoke> --arm-a <T1B smoke> --control-b <T1A smoke> --arm-b <T1B smoke> --arm-container <T1B
# container> --profile c2-packed-tp4-8x262k-ship-prefix-levern-w2-er-fx-traffic-s7 (an ABAB reader fed one boot a side: each smoke is passed twice, the boot-to-boot floors
# read exactly 1; a LOSS of 2 to 5 percent at a parity row is boot noise until a second pair says otherwise) and python3 scripts/ci/octo_compare.py --control <T1A smoke>
# --octo <T1B smoke> --octo-container <T1B container> --profile c2-packed-tp4-8x262k-ship-prefix-levern-w2-er-fx-traffic-s7 (every answer equal, user by user), python3
# scripts/ci/agent_turns.py report on both legs side by side (the per-turn answer hashes EQUAL; the returning-turn first token p50 and p90 against the control, the ship-6
# window's T1B being the baseline), python3 scripts/ci/sched2_report.py <T1B container>. UPLOAD (QWEN_PREFIX_TIER_IO_THREADS=4, QWEN_PREFIX_TIER_VERIFY_THREADS=4): the line
# "[PINDIAG] prefix: host tier upload on io_threads=4 io_chunk_blocks=.. verify_threads=4 io_wrapped=1" once, a returning session restored through the threaded IO (the
# restore line and tier_io_write_blocks above 0 in the counters), NO "host tier upload latched off" line and NO "host tier upload fell back" line (a latch is a FAIL: the
# call was repeated through the serial code, so the answers are right and the lever is not working), zero digest failures. ESTIMATE: a 254k restore of about 1 s falls
# toward 0.4 to 0.6 s, so a returning turn's first token (5.4 to 6.5 s) gains under 0.5 s. LEDGER TRIM (QWEN_FAST_ADMISSION_LEDGER_TRIM=1, the candidate only: the audited
# twins never trim): "[PINDIAG] admission ledger trim: the memory ledger ended after the attach" once and no per-rebind bracket lines after the attach. ESTIMATE: nothing on
# a warm process, about 1.7 s of a 15.4 s burst into a fresh process. LOOKUP (QWEN_FAST_LOOKUP_DRAFT=n3m12, the first card run of the lookup anywhere): "[LOOKUP-DRAFT]
# engaged policy=n3m12" per rebound request (build_ms in it: the index build runs on the step thread and stalls every decoding seat; about 0.07 s at the 32,768-token cap,
# ESTIMATE) and a "[LOOKUP-ROUND]" line per user per round. The target verifies every row, so the answers must be EQUAL to the control's; only the tokens a round commits
# can move. If the container log carries no engaged line the engine-core subprocess's output is not reaching it: say so, do not read the arm. The ship-6 reads stand: W3 WC:
# the "[PINDIAG] tp4 gdn wc engaged" line (conv_cores, gate_cores and z_cores above 0) once per engine start that has an M3 block, a "gdn wc audit <n> exact=True" line on
# the audited twin and no "audit mismatch"; the octo block (8 users of 8 rows) declines it with ONE "[PINDIAG] tp4 gdn wc fell back reason=8 users (WC takes 2 or 4)" line,
# which is the design (w3_wc_smoke accepts exactly that line); any other "fell back" line fails. GOVERNOR: the install line "[PINDIAG] lever N adaptive: decoders<=2
# share=0.9 ttft=120 gap_floor=8" once; every scheduler step line carries base=, target= and adaptive=; adaptive=1 exactly on the prefill steps that ran with one or two
# decoders live (and the base share 0.9, the target 120); adaptive=0 and today's 0.5/180 with none or three or more; f_eff never below base
# (c2_smoke_check.levern_adaptive_problems judges all of it; prefill_few_decoders NOT EXERCISED means no step was adaptive). The decode-gap floor (8 s) holds: no gap over
# it in the decoders of prefill_few_decoders. NO-GO for the candidate: any text difference against the control, an audit mismatch, a latch or fall-back line of any of the
# three groups, a fall-back line beyond the octo block's one WC line, a floor violation (a decode gap over 8 s), an allocator or admission failure, an eight-live window
# outside the boot-to-boot drift of the control, a loss in the restore pair, an errored or timed-out agent request. A failing lever is taken out by deleting its line from
# the ship entry of scripts/ci/fusion-wp/S7.json (and, for the ledger trim, from the waiver's levers) and regenerating (a new build): QWEN_PREFIX_TIER_IO_THREADS with
# QWEN_PREFIX_TIER_VERIFY_THREADS, QWEN_FAST_ADMISSION_LEDGER_TRIM, QWEN_FAST_LOOKUP_DRAFT. RUN WITH THE CI PAUSED: the ARC runner sets are at ZERO for this job (ORDER,
# ARC-ZERO); no build, no CI push, no other job beside it. CHECKPOINT READ (the workflow passes c2_smoke_check.py --expect-checkpoint): the container log holds "[QWEN-C2]
# checkpoint ukisai/Swift-1.5-Qwen3.8-27b@b4c84d42903a8646b25857eb2827288d92ed85a4 key=... weight_cache=.../weights-ckpt/<key> base_cache=... (the base cache is not read or
# written)" once, a "weight cache ...: found" line (the cache the Swift window built; this pack never builds it) and the worker's "[QWEN-C2] checkpoint bound ..." line; the
# models answer names ukisai/Swift-1.5-Qwen3.8-27b. TELEMETRY (C2_TELEMETRY=1, scripts/ci/card_telemetry.py): the read-only ARC sidecar samples every chip once a second
# through the smoke step; the first [TELEMETRY] line must say decode check ok. A rate difference that comes with a different loaded AICLK between the arms is the clocks,
# not the lever. Duration (estimate): 18 minutes.
C2_CARDS=quad
C2_ACTIONS=reset smoke
C2_IMAGE_TAG=tp4-s7-1
C2_PROFILE=c2-packed-tp4-8x262k-ship-prefix-levern-w2-er-fx-traffic-s7
C2_MODEL_REPO=ukisai/Swift-1.5-Qwen3.8-27b
C2_MODEL_REVISION=b4c84d42903a8646b25857eb2827288d92ed85a4
C2_SMOKE_TESTS=warmup,np1_live1,np1_live2,np1_live4,prefill_few_decoders,concurrent8_steady,agent_turns
C2_TELEMETRY=1
C2_BOX_MINUTES=45
